==============================================================================
Check 2: g(K) grows like kappa_0*(3/2-gamma)*ln(n), NOT like a power of n
==============================================================================
gamma= 0.10: predicted lambda=kappa0*(3/2-gamma)=3.1500, least-squares fitted slope (g(K) vs ln n)=2.9843, relative diff=5.26%
gamma= 0.30: predicted lambda=kappa0*(3/2-gamma)=2.7000, least-squares fitted slope (g(K) vs ln n)=2.5655, relative diff=4.98%
gamma= 0.50: predicted lambda=kappa0*(3/2-gamma)=2.2500, least-squares fitted slope (g(K) vs ln n)=2.1447, relative diff=4.68%
gamma= 0.70: predicted lambda=kappa0*(3/2-gamma)=1.8000, least-squares fitted slope (g(K) vs ln n)=1.7209, relative diff=4.40%
gamma= 0.90: predicted lambda=kappa0*(3/2-gamma)=1.3500, least-squares fitted slope (g(K) vs ln n)=1.2926, relative diff=4.25%
gamma= 0.99: predicted lambda=kappa0*(3/2-gamma)=1.1475, least-squares fitted slope (g(K) vs ln n)=1.0982, relative diff=4.30%

Interpretation: the fitted slope of g(K) against ln(n) (NOT against n
itself) should approach the predicted kappa_0*(3/2-gamma) as n grows,
confirming g(K) = Theta(ln n), i.e. e^{g(K)} = Theta(n^lambda), polynomial
in n as claimed -- consistent with what ATTEMPT.md's own Section 3.4
reports finding (~6% match to the leading-order prediction, attributed to
finite-n corrections from the dropped lower-order terms).

